>> title: Japanese Rice
>> category: Rice, Beans & Grains
>> tags: japanese, rice, staple, stovetop, side
>> counters: Japanese Home Cooking
>> aka: ご飯, gohan, gohan, 白米, hakumai, haku mai, plain steamed rice, Japanese short-grain rice, sticky rice for a rice bowl
>> pairs-with: miso-soup
>> servings: 4
>> time: 55 min
>> slack: unforgiving — the ten-minute rest is the last of the cooking: lift the lid and the steam that finishes the top layer is gone
>> step.1: Most households cook this in a rice cooker, and the machine is better at it. This is the stove version, because "put it in the rice cooker" is one operation and one operation is not a table. The proportion is the same either way.
>> step.2: rinse until it runs clear
>> step.3: soak 30 min
>> step.4: boil, then 12 min on the lowest heat
>> step.5: rest 10 min, lid on

Most households cook this in a rice cooker, and the machine is better at it. This is the stove version, because "put it in the rice cooker" is one operation and one operation is not a table. The proportion is the same either way: one part rice to 1.1 parts water by volume, and the rest at the end is part of the cooking.

Rinse @short-grain rice{2%cups}(475 mL, 370 g; Japanese or Korean, never long grain) in @cold water{6%cups}(1.4 L; poured off and replaced four or five times) ~rinse{3%min}, swirling it with your fingers until the water runs almost clear instead of milky. Chalky water is starch, and starch left on the grain glues the rice together.

Soak @&(~1)rinsed rice{} in @water{2 1/4%cups}(525 mL) in a #heavy saucepan{} ~soak{30%min}, until every grain has gone from translucent to opaque white right through. This is where the centre of the grain takes on water; skip it and the outside cooks before the middle does.

Bring @&(~1)soaked rice{} to a boil uncovered over medium-high heat, then clamp on the lid and drop to the lowest flame the burner has ~steam{12%min}. The lid stays on from here.

Rest @&(~1)rice{} off the heat with the lid still shut ~rest{10%min}, then fold it over twice from the bottom of the pan with a wetted paddle, which lets the steam out of the bottom layer before it turns it to paste.
